 ## Overview
-Half-Angle Slicing with Odd-Even Merge Sort is a GPU-based volume rendering technique for particle systems that correctly handles transparency and shadows by sorting particles along a view-dependent axis and compositing them in slices [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from standard splatting by using a hardware-accelerated odd-even merge sort to order particles relative to a "half-angle" vector (derived from the view and light directions), enabling accurate depth-based compositing for shadow casting without requiring expensive ray-marching or multiple full-scene passes [S1, unofficial].
+Half-angle slicing is a GPU-based volume rendering technique for particle systems that sorts particles along a view-dependent axis (the half-angle between the light and view directions) using an odd-even merge sort, then renders them in slices to handle transparency and shadows correctly [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard front-to-back splatting by decoupling the sorting axis from the view direction, allowing for efficient accumulation of opacity from the light's perspective to compute accurate volumetric shadows in a single pass [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Bitonic Sort](14217-bitonic-sort.md) which sorts strictly by depth, this method sorts by a projected coordinate that aligns with the light transport, enabling the "slicing" strategy where particles are rendered in batches to build a light-space opacity texture [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates in three main phases: simulation, sorting, and slicing/compositing. It assumes a particle system where each particle is defined by position and lifetime, stored in a 2D texture grid.
+The method operates in three main phases: simulation, GPU-based sorting, and slice-based rendering with opacity accumulation.
 
